<p class="MsoList" align="center"><b><font size="3" face="Verdana">10 Spot
Interview: Tony Cheng</font></b></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2">I had a chance to recently
shoot off a few questions to Tony Cheng, Development Manager for NFL Fever 2000,
who I had the chance to basically camp out and play Fever 2000 with at the
Microsoft booth:</font></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>1.<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span>First of all please state who you are and your role in the game.</b></font></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2">My name is Tony Cheng and I’m
the Development Manager in the Sport Games group at Microsoft.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>On the NFL Fever 2000 project, I am the team’s development lead.</font></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>2.<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span>How beneficial has the beta been and to what extent have you been able to
get good information to advance the final product? Anything in particular you
can relate? (No need to answer if you can’t think of anything)</b></font></p>
<p class="MsoListContinue"><font face="Verdana" size="2">The beta has proven to
be very valuable.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>It certainly had
its share of bugs, most of which we were already aware of by the time it was
release. However, there were some really good bugs reported that we may not have
found otherwise.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>Because of the
wide range of hardware out there, the bugs found in the beta helped out
configuration testing a great deal.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>In
the area of game play, the feedback validated what we thought worked well in the
game.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>It was good to see that our
beta testers agreed that our high level of quality in our animation really
enhanced the feel of the game.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>They
also confirmed that our running game needed more tuning, which I’m glad to say
has improved significantly.</font></p>
<p class="MsoListContinue"><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>3.<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span>Running was noticeably tough in the version I played at E3. I’ve heard
reports that the same is true in the beta. Can you elaborate on changes that
have been made to make running more successful?</b></font></p>
<p class="MsoListContinue"><font face="Verdana" size="2">Yes, that’s true.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>Running in the beta was difficult.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>That
part of the game was basically the same as in our E3 build.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>What we discovered was that, just like in real life, little things made a
big difference in the outcome of a play.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>For
example, a single crucial tackle made or missed could result in a loss or a
scoring gain.<span style="mso-spacerun:
yes">&nbsp; </span>Unfortunately, there wasn’t a single tweak that we could
have made to improve the running game in time for the beta. What we have done
since is to tune all the contributing factors to balance the running game.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>One nice thing about NFL Fever 2000 is that when you have a good line
blocking for you, you’ll see the holes open.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>You’ll also see them close as well.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>So, learning when and where to the ball will be very important.</font></p>
<p class="MsoListContinue"><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>4.<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span>It looks like the emphasis is squarely on action in NFL Fever 2000. In
this era of making games as Internet friendly as possible, why is it not
addressed in the first iteration of the game?</b></font></p>
<p class="MsoListContinue"><font face="Verdana" size="2">NFL Fever 2000 is
mainly focused on the action of NFL Football.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>In essence, that’s what makes the NFL exciting.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>The sport offers speed, power, strategy and teamwork, all performed by
world class athletes.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>These action
elements, we believe, make up the basic foundation of the game.<span style="mso-spacerun:
yes">&nbsp; </span>So, we wanted to get that part of the game right first.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>Once we have a solid foundation, we can build upon it.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>It would have been more difficult to do it the other way. We also
didn’t want to fall into the trap of doing too much and not get anything
right.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>I believe we made the right
decisions and I think once you play our game, you’ll agree.</font></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>5.<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span>The loss of the Front Page Sports Series has left a definite hole in
terms of realistic career mode football. Since most companies have a solid life
cycle developed in terms of what they plan to achieve for the future of the
series, what type of growth for the series do you foresee?</b></font></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2">Career mode is probably the
single most significant feature that made the Front Page Sports Football series
great.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>While we didn’t include
this feature in our very first version, you can bet this is an area that we will
be concentrating on in future versions.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>We
believe so much in this that we recently recruited Pat Cook, a principal
designer of the original Front Page Sports series, responsible for pioneering
features like career mode in sports games.</font></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>6.<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span>In that same vein of foreseeing the future, people who play games love
records and love breaking them even more. For myself, the chance to go and look
back at past statistics for a previous season was awe-inspiring. With the
enormous capacity of storage that most gamers, casual and hardcore alike have,
what’s your take on having these features in later iterations?</b></font></p>
<p class="MsoListContinue"><font face="Verdana" size="2">Statistics are very
important part of the NFL as with most professional sports.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>In order for us to do a good job with features like career mode in our
future versions, it will be a requirement that we record all the appropriate and
necessary statistics.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>When records
are broken in the NFL, it’s a big deal, and likewise, they shall be in future
editions of NFL Fever.</font></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>7.<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span>Any word on being able to see stats during the game, with a hot key or
any other method. Also at the end of the game as well.</b></font></p>
<p class="MsoListContinue"><font face="Verdana" size="2">We have game summary
stats presented at the end of each quarter and the end of the game.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>More powerful ways to view detail statistics during the game is something
our users can look forward to in the future versions of NFL Fever.</font></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>8.<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span>The game is definitely beautiful from what I can see so far, what steps
were taken to make the players look so lifelike?</b></font></p>
<p class="MsoListContinue"><font face="Verdana" size="2">Because our focus is to
get the on field action right, the authentic look of the players is critical.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>Our art team worked painstakingly to create different body styles of
players to accurately reflect their real life counterparts.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>The players moved realistically because we motion captured real players
that played the various positions in football.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>Moreover, we invested a lot of energy to ensure that we kept the motions
pure and blended them seamlessly together.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>I believe the quality of our player movements will be difficult to
surpass.</font></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>9.<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span>What’s your favorite “thing” about the game?</b></font></p>
<p class="MsoListContinue"><font face="Verdana" size="2">There are a lot of very
cool things we’ve put into NFL Fever 2000.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>My favorite thing in game is probably the lower-the-shoulder move.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>When I’m in control of a big strong back like Jamal Anderson, you’ll
see me smiling ear to ear when I’m about to mow down some fly-weight defensive
back to break for a long run.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>To
top that off, I will high step my way into the end zone just to rub it in.</font></p>
<p class="MsoList"><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>10.<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&nbsp;
</span>That’s it for me... anything you’d like to add?</b></font></p>
<p class="MsoListContinue"><font face="Verdana" size="2">We spent a great deal
of time working with NFL coaches to develop realistic and credible AI for all
the players.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>So, not only do our
players look and move realistically, they play their positions realistically.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>The on field action is augmented by rich sounds of bone-crunching hits
and an interactive home team crowd. <span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;</span>The
gaming experience is further enhanced by the play-by-play and the color
commentary of Dick Stockton and Matt Millen respectively.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp;
</span>This is Microsoft’s first effort in creating an exciting and fun NFL
Football game for the PC.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">&nbsp; </span>While
there are many new improvements to come, NFL Fever 2000, I believe, will keep
its of being a fast, hard-hitting and easy to play football game.</font></p>
<p><font face="Verdana" size="2">Thanks Tony for taking time out to answer a few
questions!</font></p>
